anted outcome.</li></ul>\n<h2>5. Example</h2><p>Imagine a student working in a small, isolated practice environment. The student records the starting conditions, follows the documented steps for this topic, observes the result, and compares it with the expected behaviour. If the result differs, the student checks one setting at a time and records the correction. No third-party system or private data is involved.</p>\n<h2>6. Practical learning activity</h2><ol><li>Write a one-sentence definition of IP Addressing — IPv4 and IPv6.</li><li>Draw a simple diagram showing its main components or stages.</li><li>Use a classroom example or approved simulator to observe the concept.</li><li>Record the expected result, actual result, and what you learned.</li></ol>\n<h2>7. Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Memorizing terms without understanding the workflow.</li><li>Assuming one example applies to every environment.</li><li>Changing several settings at once, making results difficult to explain.</li><li>Testing systems or data without permission.</li></ul>\n<h2>8. Key points</h2><ul><li>Understand the purpose before focusing on tools.</li><li>Verify results instead of relying on assumptions.</li><li>Document important steps and limitations.</li><li>Practice only in approved learning environments.</li></ul>\n<h2>9. Practice questions</h2><ol><li>Define IP Addressing — IPv4 and IPv6 in your own words.</li><li>State two reasons this topic is useful.</li><li>Explain the main components or stages.</li><li>Give one example and describe how you would verify the result.</li><li>What mistakes should a beginner avoid?</li></ol>\n<h2>10. Summary</h2><p>This lesson introduced IP Addressing — IPv4 and IPv6, its purpose, terminology, and a safe way to practise. Review the key points and try explaining the concept without looking at the notes.</p>\n</article>',6) ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE lesson_id=VALUES(lesson_id), title=VALUES(title), content=VALUES(content), topic_order=VALUES(topic_order);
