INSERT INTO topics (id,lesson_id,title,content,topic_order) VALUES (90,9,'Secure Configuration Assessment','<article class="cv-topic-content">\n<h2>1. Introduction</h2><p><strong>Secure Configuration Assessment</strong> is part of <strong>Advanced Learning Labs</strong>. This lesson explains the core idea, why it matters, and how it fits into real computing and security work. Begin with the concept, then connect it to examples and safe practice.</p>\n<h2>2. Learning objectives</h2><ul><li>Define the main terms related to secure configuration assessment.</li><li>Explain the purpose and basic working principles.</li><li>Recognize common use cases, limitations, and mistakes.</li><li>Apply the concept in a classroom, simulator, or system you own or are explicitly authorized to use.</li></ul>\n<h2>3. Detailed explanation</h2><p>Start by identifying the inputs, the process, and the expected output. In a computer system, this topic is connected to how information is represented, transferred, processed, or protected. A reliable understanding includes both the normal workflow and the conditions under which it can fail.</p><p>When studying <strong>Secure Configuration Assessment</strong>, compare the main components and note what each component is responsible for. Look at the sequence of events rather than memorizing isolated definitions. Consider performance, reliability, privacy, and security where they apply. In professional environments, decisions should be based on documented requirements, verified evidence, and the principle of least privilege.</p>\n<h2>4. Important terminology</h2><ul><li><strong>Concept:</strong> the central idea explained in this lesson.</li><li><strong>Component:</strong> one part of a larger system or workflow.</li><li><strong>Configuration:</strong> the settings that control how a component behaves.</li><li><strong>Validation:</strong> checking that an expected result is correct.</li><li><strong>Risk:</strong> the possibility and impact of an unwa